# Bootstrap for S3 Access

## Simple Step-by-Step Guide

Use this script as **EC2 User Data / bootstrap script**. The commands are kept in the same order so the server can set itself up automatically when the instance starts.

---

## Step 1 — Create an EC2 Instance

Launch an Ubuntu EC2 instance.

Attach an IAM Role that has permission to read the required S3 bucket.

Also allow **HTTP Port 80** in the EC2 Security Group if you want to open the Apache website in a browser.

---

## Step 2 — Open User Data

During EC2 launch, open:

**Advanced details → User data**

Paste the complete script shown below.

---

## Step 3 — Bootstrap Script

```bash
#!/bin/bash

apt update -y
apt install apache2 unzip curl -y

# Download AWS CLI v2
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"

# Extract AWS CLI
unzip -q awscliv2.zip

# Install AWS CLI
./aws/install

# Copy files from S3 bucket to Apache web directory
aws s3 cp s3://rick-buck /var/www/html/ --recursive

# Enable and start Apache
systemctl enable apache2
systemctl start apache2
```

---

## Step 4 — What the Script Does

1. Updates Ubuntu packages.
2. Installs Apache, unzip and curl.
3. Downloads AWS CLI v2.
4. Extracts and installs AWS CLI.
5. Copies files from the S3 bucket to `/var/www/html/`.
6. Enables Apache to start automatically.
7. Starts the Apache web server.

---

## Step 5 — Launch the Instance

Finish creating the EC2 instance and wait until it is running.

The User Data script runs automatically during the first boot.

---

## Step 6 — Verify the Website

Copy the EC2 **Public IPv4 address** and open:

```text
http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP
```

If the S3 files were copied successfully, the website content should appear.

---

## Important

- The EC2 instance needs permission to access the S3 bucket.
- Prefer an **IAM Role** instead of putting AWS Access Keys inside the script.
- Replace the example bucket name only when you want to use a different S3 bucket.
- Make sure HTTP Port 80 is allowed in the Security Group.
